{"step_index":282,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-24T17:53:42Z","input_tokens":2367,"cache_read_tokens":161699,"output_tokens":3326,"thinking":"Processing of the extensive log data is complete, enabling a deeper examination of the evidence. Initial analysis reveals the presence of several PDF documents, alongside SQL database files.\n\nFocus shifts to understanding the structure of the evidence records, specifically the data extracted from `customer_data_inventory_*.sql` files. Attention is on the fields extracted from the `customer_profiles` table, including `resource_id` and `customer` data.\n\n\nAnalyzing data structures within the `customer_data_inventory_*.sql` files to understand which data fields constitute policy violations. Identifying specific patterns in the data, such as account status or the presence of specific identifiers, triggers different violation categories.  Hypothesizing the need to flag records based on the identified criteria, considering potential false positives and edge cases, requiring further validation steps.\n\n\nAnalyzing each data structure to determine potential policy violations, starting with `connector_subscriptions`. Checking for active data flows by examining the \"enabled\" field.  Investigating `backup_restore_entries` for records with restore risks based on the \"restore_allowed\" status.\n\nReviewing `security_audit_records` to verify the retention policy compliance based on the `retention_until` field. Considering that the other data extracts will require detailed parsing of JSON and SQL structures to pull out record attributes.  Proceeding to interpret the SQL schema to extract relevant fields for each rule.\n\n\nAnalyzing additional data sources to correlate record presence across different services. Evaluating the contents of `service_resource_export_*.json` and `customer_account_handover_*.txt` to identify discrepancies in record status. Recognizing that `NOT_FOUND` in the handover files indicates an `ABSENT` record. The lookup files provide verification of record presence, while audit logs require a confirmation of `PRESENT` records.\n\n\nMapping case identifiers to their associated resources within the output file reveals a one-to-one correspondence between cases and their respective evidence. Verification involves confirming each case's steps are completely represented within its cited evidence. For instance, `case_01` cites `evidence_54`, with all described steps of the case now confirmed to exist within the evidence. Further analysis of the output file would confirm the complete set of cited steps.\n\n\nA recurring pattern emerges: each case always includes an account record alongside other service resources. Specifically, every case references an `account_service` record. The remaining services cited differ case by case, indicating these might be secondary resources associated with account data. The account records consistently have associated services.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"python3 -c \\\"\\nimport pandas as pd, json, os, glob, sqlite3, re\\n\\n# Let's write an evidence indexer that indexes all evidence files\\nmanifest = pd.read_csv('dataset/evidence_files.csv').set_index('file_id')\\nmanifest['cap_dt'] = pd.to_datetime(manifest['captured_at'])\\n\\n# Load users and audit log\\nusers_df = pd.read_csv('dataset/user_identities.csv')\\naudit_df = pd.read_csv('dataset/user_audit_log.csv')\\n\\nuser_graph = {}\\nemail_to_uid = {}\\nfor _, u in users_df.iterrows():\\n    uid = u['user_id']\\n    user_graph[uid] = {\\n        'user_id': uid,\\n        'emails': {str(u['email']).strip().lower()} if pd.notna(u['email']) else set(),\\n        'phones': {str(u['phone']).strip()} if pd.notna(u['phone']) else set(),\\n        'hashes': {str(u['email_hash']).strip().lower()} if pd.notna(u['email_hash']) else set(),\\n        'names': {str(u['name']).strip().lower()} if pd.notna(u['name']) else set(),\\n    }\\n    if pd.notna(u['email']):\\n        email_to_uid[str(u['email']).strip().lower()] = uid\\n\\nfor _, a in audit_df.iterrows():\\n    uid = a['user_id']\\n    if uid not in user_graph:\\n        user_graph[uid] = {'user_id': uid, 'emails': set(), 'phones': set(), 'hashes': set(), 'names': set()}\\n    fn = a['field_name']\\n    for v in [a['old_value'], a['new_value']]:\\n        if pd.notna(v):\\n            vs = str(v).strip()\\n            if fn == 'email':\\n                user_graph[uid]['emails'].add(vs.lower())\\n                email_to_uid[vs.lower()] = uid\\n            elif fn == 'phone':\\n                user_graph[uid]['phones'].add(vs)\\n            elif fn == 'name':\\n                user_graph[uid]['names'].add(vs.lower())\\n\\nprint(f'User graph built for {len(user_graph)} users.')\\n\\\"\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolSummary":"\"Verify identity graph logic\""}}]}
